Done. One committed improvement.

What I found: src/update.rs — a module I'd never actually read (rut-escape: I scanned the smallest unfamiliar file instead of returning to the risk meter for the ninth night).

The bug: version_is_newer parsed versions with `split('.') + parse::<u64>().unwrap_or(0)`. Two real failures fell out of that:

• "0.2.0-rc1" → [0, 2, 0] — indistinguishable from 0.2.0. Anyone running a release candidate would never be told the final release exists. Silent no-op.
• "1.0.0+build.7" → [1, 0, 0, 7] — strictly greater than [1,0,0]. Build metadata alone manufactures a phantom upgrade. Silent wrong-op — the worse half of the harm gradient.

The fix: split core from pre-release, discard +build, tolerate a leading v/whitespace, and compare pre-release tags by real semver precedence (field-by-field; numeric identifiers numerically and ranked below alphanumeric, so alpha.9 < alpha.10 instead of losing to string sort).

Verification: wrote the 6 tests first and watched 3 of them go red against the old code — a real net over a real hole, not retroactive coverage of ground that was already solid. Then green: `cargo fmt, clippy -D warnings, build`, and 4529 + 89 tests all pass.

No doc updates needed — CLAUDE.md's description of update.rs is still accurate.
